tel 1.0

base hellenism
import soundness

info   the JVM classpath, its classloaders and its resources as typed values
detail
    _Hellenism_ represents the three things the JVM uses to find code and data — the
    **classpath** (an ordered list of directories, JAR files and the Java runtime), the
    **classloaders** which search it, and the **resources** bundled on it — as immutable,
    typed values, in place of the standard API's nullable lookups and mutable global state.

    A resource is a path on the `Classpath` plane, read with the same `read[Data]` or
    `read[Text]` as any other source, and the `cp"…"` interpolator checks that the resource
    exists while the code compiles. A classpath is a value whose `Classpath.Entry`s can be
    listed and matched, and a classloader is a `Classloader` value, chosen explicitly as a
    contextual value from the `classloaders` package rather than reached for through the
    thread's mutable state.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem

    val bytes: Data = cp"/scala/Option.class".read[Data]
    val classpath: LocalClasspath = System.properties.java.`class`.path().as[LocalClasspath]
    val loader: Classloader = classpath.classloader(Classloader.Delegation.Deferential)
    ```

    The library is split into three source sets, by what each platform can do:

    - `hellenism.core` — `Classpath`, `Resource`, `cp"…"`, `Classloader`, `ClassRef` and the
      `classloaders` package. Compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native; on Native a
      program's classpath is linked into its binary, and `cp"…"` resources reach the
      javalib's embedded resources.
    - `hellenism.core` on the JVM and Scala.js additionally has the extensions which need
      `java.net.URL`, `URLClassLoader` and `ServiceLoader`: `classloader`, `services`,
      `javaUrl` and `classpathEntry`.
    - `hellenism.jvm` — `LocalClasspath` and everything backed by the filesystem:
      `classpathSubstantiable`, `Classloader#classpath` and `Classpath.of`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `classloaders` choice
    package merges with the umbrella's own, so a user writes `import soundness.*` and then
    imports one classloader by name.

refer hellenism.Classpath
refer hellenism.Classloader
refer hellenism.LocalClasspath

entry .Classloader   a Java `ClassLoader` as a value: resolves names, reads resources, scopes itself
  detail
      A `Classloader` wraps a `java.lang.ClassLoader`, whose raw instance is `#java`. It is
      the context every resource read resolves against — the `Streamable` givens for
      `Resource` and for a `Path on Classpath` take one as a contextual parameter — and is
      chosen by importing one of the `classloaders` package's givens by name, or by building
      one over a `Classpath` with `classloader`.

      Two classloaders may resolve the same path to different resources, or one to nothing at
      all, which is why the choice is never implicit: a `Classloader` is always a value or a
      named given in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      val loader: Classloader = Classloader[Option[Int]]
      loader.parent
      loader.on(t"scala.Option")
      loader.use:
        Classloader.threadContext == loader
      ```

      A classloader is `Findable`, with the `Classpath` as its `#Plane`, so a `Path on
      Classpath` can be tested for existence against it (`classpathSubstantiable`, in
      `hellenism.jvm`).
  refer hellenism.classloaders
  refer hellenism.classloader
  entry .threadContext   the current thread's context classloader, as a value
      The classloader `Thread.currentThread.getContextClassLoader` returns at the moment of
      the call. `classloaders.threadContextClassloader` is the given built from it, and
      `#use` installs another classloader as the thread's context for the duration of a block.
  entry .apply   the classloader which loaded a type, as `Classloader[T]`
      `Classloader[T]` is a macro which finds the class of `T` through `ClassRef[T]` and
      returns the classloader which loaded it. This is how a plugin's own classloader is
      reached from one of its classes, and how a program finds the classpath it was itself
      loaded from (`LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Main.type])`), which under a host such as a
      test runner differs from the `java.class.path` property.

      The type may be a proper type, an object's type or an unapplied type constructor, and
      may be nested in an object or a class: the macro resolves it to its JVM binary name
      (`Outer$Nested`), where a lookup by the dotted source name would fail.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Outer:
        class Nested

      Classloader[Outer.Nested].java == classOf[Outer.Nested].getClassLoader
      ```
  entry .Delegation   which side wins when a loader and its parent both offer a class
    detail
        A classloader built over a classpath with `classloader` must say how it resolves a
        name which both it and its parent can load. The JVM's own order consults the parent
        first; the reverse prefers the loader's own entries, which isolates a plugin's
        versions of shared libraries from its host's. The choice is always spelled out, since
        the two give different classes for the same name and neither is right for every use.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val contract = LocalClasspath(Classpath.Entry.Jar(t"/lib/contract.jar"))
        val plugin = LocalClasspath(Classpath.Entry.Jar(t"/lib/plugin.jar"))
        val shared = contract.classloader(Classloader.Delegation.Deferential)
        val isolated = plugin.classloader(Classloader.Delegation.Preferential, parent = shared)
        ```

        Here the plugin sees the contract it was written against through its parent, while
        everything else it bundles is its own.

        `Deferential` is parent-first, the JVM's standard order: the loader asks its parent
        for every name before looking in its own entries, which is what a plain
        `URLClassLoader` does, so a class the parent can load is shared with the code being
        loaded. `Preferential` is child-first: the loader looks in its own entries first and
        defers to its parent only for a name it cannot find, so the classpath's versions of
        shared libraries shadow the parent's. A `Preferential` loader still honours the
        `ClassLoader` contract: it consults `findLoadedClass` under the per-name loading lock
        before defining a class, so a second request for the same name, or two threads
        racing on it, never reaches `defineClass` twice.
  entry #java   the underlying `java.lang.ClassLoader`
      The raw Java classloader, for the Java APIs which take one. Two `Classloader`s are the
      same loader when their `java` instances are.
  entry #Plane   the plane this classloader finds paths on: `Classpath`
  entry #parent   the parent classloader, or `Unset` for the bootstrap loader
      Every JVM classloader but the bootstrap loader has a parent it delegates to; this walks
      one step up the chain, wrapped as a `Classloader`.
  entry #use   runs a block with this as the thread's context classloader
      Installs this classloader as the current thread's context classloader, evaluates the
      block, and restores the previous one in a `finally`, so a change of classloader is
      scoped to exactly the code which needs it — loading a plugin, or calling a Java library
      which reads the context loader — rather than left set for the thread.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      classloaders.systemClassloader.use:
        Classloader[Option[Int]]
      ```
  entry #on   loads and initialises a class by its binary name, if this loader has it
      `Class.forName(name, true, java)`: the class is initialised, and the result is `Unset`
      rather than a thrown `ClassNotFoundException` when the loader cannot find it. The name
      is the JVM binary name, with `$` between a nested class and its owner.
  entry #resource   the bytes of a resource, or `Unset` if this loader does not have it
      Reads the whole of `getResourceAsStream(path)` and closes the stream. The path is
      relative, without a leading `/`, as the Java API takes it. The method takes no
      capability, so its result retains nothing and the read may sit inside a `safely` block;
      `#apply` is the same read with the result logged.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      safely(threadContextClassloader.resource(t"scala/Option.class")).let(_.length)
      ```
  entry #apply   the bytes of a resource, logged as a `Classpath.Event` when found
      As `#resource`, and logs `Classpath.Event.ResourceLoaded` at `fine` level when the
      resource is present. It needs a `Classpath.Event is Loggable` in scope, such as
      `logging.silentLogging`.

entry .Classpath   the root of the classpath plane, and a classpath as a list of entries
  detail
      `Classpath` is two things which share a name. As a **type**, it is a classpath: an
      ordered list of `#entries`, each a `Classpath.Entry`, which `LocalClasspath` and
      `OnlineClasspath` implement. As an **object**, it is the root of the classpath
      **plane**, so a resource is a `Path on Classpath` built from it:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val path: Path on Classpath = Classpath / "scala" / "Option.class"
      val same: Path on Classpath = t"/scala/Option.class".as[Path on Classpath]
      path.read[Data]
      ```

      A path on the classpath encodes as `/`-separated text with no root prefix, each segment
      held to `.Rules`: no `/`, not beginning with a digit, and only letters, digits, `_`,
      `$` and `.`. The path is read through the `Classloader` given in scope (`.streamable`),
      and raises `Classpath.Error` if that loader does not have it.

      On the JVM, the extensions `classloader` and `services` build a classloader over a
      classpath and enumerate its service providers; `Classpath.of` reads one back from a
      `URLClassLoader`.
  refer hellenism.LocalClasspath
  refer hellenism.OnlineClasspath
  refer hellenism.Resource
  entry .Plane   the plane type of classpath paths: `Classpath` itself
  entry .Rules   what a segment of a classpath path may be
      A segment must not contain `/`, must not begin with a digit, and must match
      `[a-zA-Z0-9_$.]+`: the characters a class file or package directory name may use. A
      path which breaks a rule fails to decode with a `Path.Error`.
  entry .nominative   the given which holds classpath paths to `.Rules`
  entry .radical   how the classpath root is read from and written to text
      The root encodes as the empty text, so a path reads as `/scala/Option.class`; decoding
      accepts only text beginning with `/`, and fails with `Path.Error(InvalidRoot)` otherwise.
  entry .filesystem   the classpath as a `Filesystem`: `/`-separated, with one root
      Named `Java classpath`, with `/` as its separator and `.` and `..` as the self and
      parent segments, and a single root, so a path on the classpath is always absolute.
  entry .Directory   makes a `Classpath.Entry.Directory` from any path type
      `Classpath.Directory(path)` takes any value which is `Abstractable across Paths to
      Text` — a galilei `Path`, or text — and makes the directory entry for it.
  entry .Jar   makes a `Classpath.Entry.Jar` from any path type
      `Classpath.Jar(path)` takes any value which is `Abstractable across Paths to Text` and
      makes the JAR entry for it.
  entry .streamable   reads a `Path on Classpath` as bytes, through the `Classloader` in scope
      The given which lets a classpath path be `read[Data]` (or `read[Text]`, or as any type
      decodable from a stream). It needs a `Classloader` given and a `Tactic[Classpath.Error]`,
      which is raised when the classloader does not have the resource.
  entry .source   reads a `Path on Classpath` as a stream metered by `Credit`
      As `.streamable`, with a `Buffering` in scope, for reading the resource lazily as a
      stream rather than all at once.
  entry .Entry   one entry of a classpath: a directory, a JAR, a URL or the Java runtime
    detail
        A sealed hierarchy of the places a classpath entry can point to. A `LocalClasspath`
        holds only `Directory`, `Jar` and `JavaRuntime` entries, so a match over its entries
        need not handle `Url`; an `OnlineClasspath` may hold any.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val entries: List[Classpath.Entry] =
          List(Classpath.Entry.Jar(t"/lib/app.jar"), Classpath.Entry.Directory(t"/classes/"))

        entries.map:
          case Classpath.Entry.Directory(path) => t"directory $path"
          case Classpath.Entry.Jar(path)       => t"archive $path"
          case Classpath.Entry.Url(url)        => t"remote $url"
          case Classpath.Entry.JavaRuntime     => t"the Java runtime"
        ```

        On the JVM, `javaUrl` converts any entry to the `java.net.URL` a `URLClassLoader`
        takes.
    entry .Directory   a directory of class files and resources, by its path
      entry #path    the directory's path, as text
      entry #apply   the directory as a path type of the caller's choosing
          `entry[Path on Linux]()` (or any `Instantiable across Paths from Text`) makes a
          typed path from the entry's text.
    entry .Jar   a JAR file, by its path
      entry #path    the archive's path, as text
      entry #apply   the archive as a path type of the caller's choosing
    entry .Url   an entry fetched over the network, by its URL
      entry #url     the URL, as text
      entry #apply   the entry as a URL type of the caller's choosing
          `entry[HttpUrl]()` (or any `Instantiable across Urls from Text`) makes a typed URL
          from the entry's text.
    entry .JavaRuntime   the JDK's own modules, the `jrt:/` filesystem
        The entry which supplies `java.lang.String` and the rest of the JDK. It has no path:
        a `URLClassLoader` cannot load from `jrt:/`, so `classloader` omits it and relies on
        the platform loader at the root of every parent chain, and `LocalClasspath#apply`
        drops it from the text it renders.
  entry .Error   raised when a resource is not on the classpath
    detail
        Error 540: _the resource `path` was not on the classpath_. Raised by the `Streamable`
        givens for `Resource` and `Path on Classpath` when the `Classloader` in scope does
        not have the resource. A `cp"…"` resource is checked at compile time against the
        compiler's classpath, so this error at runtime means the program was launched with a
        different one.
    entry #resource   the path which was asked for
  entry .Event   what a classloader logs as it reads resources
    detail
        Every `Event` is tagged `Log.Runtime` and `Log.Resource`, so a logging configuration
        can select or silence them by tag. `Classloader#apply` logs `ResourceLoaded` at
        `fine`, and a failed read logs `ResourceMissing` as a warning before raising
        `Classpath.Error`. A `Classpath.Event is Loggable` must be in scope for the logged
        reads; `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
    entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
    entry .ResourceLoaded   a resource was read: _loaded the classpath resource `path`_
      entry #path   the resource's path
    entry .ResourceMissing   a resource was not found: _the classpath resource `path` was not found_
      entry #path   the resource's path
  entry #entries   the classpath's entries, in search order

entry .OnlineClasspath   a classpath whose entries may be fetched over the network
  detail
      A `Classpath` of any `Classpath.Entry`s, including `Url`s. `Classpath.of` builds one
      from a `URLClassLoader` whose URLs include an `http` or `https` one; a classpath of
      local entries alone is a `LocalClasspath`, whose entries are typed to exclude URLs.
  refer hellenism.LocalClasspath
  entry #entries   the entries, in search order

entry .Resource   a resource on the classpath, named by `cp"…"` and checked at compile time
  detail
      A `Resource` is a `Path on Classpath` which the compiler has confirmed exists: the only
      way to make one is the `cp"…"` interpolator, whose macro looks the path up on the
      compiler's own classpath. Its type carries the path as the literal type `Locus`, so
      `cp"/data/table.csv"` is a `Resource { type Locus = "/data/table.csv" }`.

      A resource reads like any other source, through the `Classloader` given in scope:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val data: Data = cp"/scala/Option.class".read[Data]
      ```

      The runtime classloader may still lack what the compiler's classpath had, in which case
      the read raises `Classpath.Error`.
  refer hellenism.cp
  entry .streamable   reads a `Resource` as bytes, through the `Classloader` in scope
      The given which lets a resource be `read[Data]`, `read[Text]`, or read as any type
      decodable from a stream. The `Classpath.Error` for a missing resource is thrown
      unscoped rather than raised through a tactic, since a `cp"…"` resource has been checked
      at compile time.
  entry .source   reads a `Resource` as a stream metered by `Credit`
      As `.streamable`, with a `Buffering` in scope, for reading the resource lazily.
  entry .nominable   a resource's name: the first segment of its path, or `/`
  entry #path   the resource's path on the classpath plane

entry .cp   the `cp"…"` interpolator: a classpath resource, checked as the code compiles
    `cp"/path/to/resource"` names a resource on the classpath and checks, while the code
    compiles, that it exists on the compiler's classpath (through the compiler's thread
    context classloader). The result is a `Resource` whose `Locus` type member is the path's
    literal type. A path which is not a valid classpath path is a compile error, _hellenism:
    the path `foobar` is not a valid classpath path_, and one which exists nowhere on the
    compiler's classpath is another, _hellenism: the path `/missing.txt` is not on the
    classpath_.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    cp"/scala/Option.class".read[Data].length
    ```

    The path should begin with `/`, as a classpath path is always absolute; the macro
    tolerates one without, but the `Classpath` plane does not decode such text. The
    interpolator takes no `$`-substitutions: the path must be a literal, since it is checked
    at compile time.

entry .classloaders   the standard classloaders, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which classloader resolves a resource is decided by the `Classloader` given in scope,
      and the choice is deliberately never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of
      these in, and one is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      ```

      The thread's context classloader suits most applications. The system and platform
      loaders are the JVM's own, and `scalaClassloader` is whichever loaded the Scala
      library. A classloader built from a `Classpath` with `classloader` can be bound as a
      local given instead.
  entry .threadContextClassloader   the current thread's context classloader
      Built from `Classloader.threadContext` when the given is first used, so it is the
      context loader of the thread which first asked for it.
  entry .systemClassloader   the JVM's system (application) classloader
      `ClassLoader.getSystemClassLoader`: the loader over the `java.class.path` property, at
      the top of the application's delegation chain.
  entry .platformClassloader   the JVM's platform classloader
      `ClassLoader.getPlatformClassLoader`: the loader of the JDK's own modules, and the
      default parent of a classloader built with `classloader`.
  entry .scalaClassloader   the classloader which loaded the Scala library
      `Classloader[List]`: the loader the Scala standard library came from, which is the
      application loader in a plain `java -cp` process and the host's under a plugin system.

entry .ClassRef   a `Class[?]` found from a type, as `ClassRef[T]`
    `ClassRef` is the opaque type `hellenism.internal.ClassRef`, exported here. `ClassRef[T]`
    is a macro which resolves a type to its runtime class: a proper type is linked with
    `classOf`, binding the class the caller itself links against, and an unapplied type
    constructor is looked up by its JVM binary name. A type nested in an object or a class
    resolves to its binary name (`Outer$Nested`), where `Class.forName` on the dotted source
    name would fail. `ClassRef(javaClass)` wraps a class already in hand.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    object Outer:
      class Nested

    ClassRef[Outer.Nested].getName.nn.tt == t"Outer$$Nested"
    ClassRef[Outer.type].getName.nn.tt
    ClassRef[scala.Option].getName.nn.tt == t"scala.Option"
    ```

    A `ClassRef` is a `Class[?]`, so every Java method of a class is available on it. Its
    `classloader` extension gives the `Classloader` which loaded it, and on the JVM
    `classpathEntry` gives the `Classpath.Entry` it was loaded from. It inspects as the source
    which would produce it, `classOf[java.lang.String]`, rather than the two-word
    `class java.lang.String` of `Class#toString`.

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `cp"…"`, `ClassRef[T]` and `Classloader[T]`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home. `ClassRef`
      is exported from here to the `hellenism` package.
  entry .ClassRef   a `Class[?]` found from a type; see `hellenism.ClassRef`
    detail
        The opaque type `ClassRef <: Class[?]`, with the macro `ClassRef[T]` and the wrapper
        `ClassRef(javaClass)`. Its page under the `hellenism` package has the full account.
    entry .apply   `ClassRef[T]` resolves a type to its class; `ClassRef(cls)` wraps one
        The macro form takes a type of any kind: a proper type is linked with `classOf`, an
        unapplied type constructor is looked up by its JVM binary name, and a type with no
        class at all is a compile error, _hellenism: the type `T` does not have a class_.
    entry .inspectable   renders a class reference as `classOf[java.lang.String]`
  entry .classloader   the classloader which loaded a `ClassRef`'s class
      An extension on `ClassRef`: `classRef.getClassLoader`, wrapped as a `Classloader`.
      `Classloader[T]` is `ClassRef[T].classloader`.
  entry .classpath   the macro behind `cp"…"`
      Reads the interpolator's one literal part, decodes it as a `Path on Classpath`, looks it
      up on the compiler's context classloader, and splices a `Resource` whose `Locus` type is
      the literal.

entry .Hellenism2   the trait which holds the `ClassRef[T]` macro implementation
  hidden
  detail
      Mixed into `internal`; not for direct use.
  entry #makeClass   the macro behind `ClassRef[T]`: a type to its class, by `classOf` or binary name

entry .classloader   builds a classloader over a classpath, with a chosen delegation and parent
  detail
      A JVM-only extension on `Classpath` (absent on Scala Native, where the classpath is
      linked into the binary). It builds a `URLClassLoader` over the classpath's entries —
      `JavaRuntime` is omitted, since a `URLClassLoader` cannot load from `jrt:/` and the
      platform loader at the root of every chain supplies the JDK anyway — delegating to
      `parent` as `delegation` says.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val plugin = LocalClasspath(Classpath.Entry.Jar(t"/lib/plugin.jar"))
      val loader = plugin.classloader(Classloader.Delegation.Preferential)
      loader.parent.let(_.java == ClassLoader.getPlatformClassLoader.nn)
      ```

      The delegation is required: `Deferential` is the JVM's parent-first order, which shares
      the parent's classes with what is loaded; `Preferential` is child-first, which isolates
      the classpath's own versions of its libraries from the parent's. See
      `Classloader.Delegation`. The parent is the platform classloader
      (`classloaders.platformClassloader`) unless another is given.

entry .services   the providers of a service interface declared on a classpath
  detail
      A JVM-only extension on `Classpath`. `classpath.services[Service]` finds every
      implementation of `Service` registered under `META-INF/services/<Service>` on the
      classpath's entries, through `java.util.ServiceLoader`, instantiates each, and returns
      them as a `Set` typed to the service. The lookup uses a fresh `URLClassLoader` over the
      entries whose parent is the loader of the service class itself (or the system loader,
      for a JDK interface), so a provider is linked against the same `Service` the caller
      named; providers are deduplicated by their class.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val classpath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
      classpath.services[java.nio.file.spi.FileSystemProvider].map(_.getScheme.nn.tt)
      ```

      The `Set` is unordered: a service whose providers must be ranked needs a key of its
      own.

entry .javaUrl   a classpath entry as the `java.net.URL` a `URLClassLoader` takes
    A JVM-only extension on `Classpath.Entry`. A `Directory` or `Jar` becomes a `file:` URL
    from its path, a `Url` is parsed as written, and `JavaRuntime` is `jrt:/`.

entry .classpathEntry   the classpath entry a class was loaded from, if it is known
    A JVM-only extension on `ClassRef`. Reads the class's protection domain's code source
    location and classifies it as a `Classpath.Entry`: a `file:` URL ending in `/` is a
    `Directory`, any other `file:` URL a `Jar`, `http` and `https` a `Url`, and `jrt` the
    `JavaRuntime`. A URL of any other protocol is `Unset`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    ClassRef[Option[Int]].classpathEntry
    ```

entry .classpathSubstantiable   tests whether a `Path on Classpath` exists, through the `Classloader` in scope
    The `Substantiable` given for classpath paths, in `hellenism.jvm`: `path.existent()` is
    whether the classloader in scope has a resource at that path, so a program can check for
    an optional resource rather than handle a `Classpath.Error`. It is a top-level given, so
    it must be imported by name: `import soundness.classpathSubstantiable`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.classpathSubstantiable
    import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

    val path: Path on Classpath = Classpath / "scala" / "Option.class"
    path.existent()
    ```

entry .classpath   the classpath a classloader loads from, if it is a `URLClassLoader`
    An extension on `Classloader` in `hellenism.jvm`. Walks the loader and its parents to the
    nearest `URLClassLoader` and reads its URLs back as a `Classpath` with `Classpath.of`; a
    chain with no `URLClassLoader` in it gives `Unset`. Under a host such as a test runner,
    which isolates each suite in a `URLClassLoader` of its own, this is the classpath the
    suite's classes actually came from, where the `java.class.path` property names the
    host's jars; `LocalClasspath.of(classloader)` combines the two.

entry .of   reads a `Classpath` back from a `URLClassLoader`'s URLs, as `Classpath.of`
    An extension on the `Classpath` object, in `hellenism.jvm`. Each URL becomes a
    `Classpath.Entry` by its protocol (`file:` to a `Directory` or `Jar`, `http`/`https` to a
    `Url`, `jrt` to `JavaRuntime`; any other protocol is dropped). The result is an
    `OnlineClasspath` if any entry is a `Url`, and otherwise a `LocalClasspath`, whose
    entries are typed to exclude URLs.

entry .LocalClasspath   a classpath of local entries alone: directories, JARs and the Java runtime
  detail
      The classpath of a running program, as a value. Its `#entries` are typed as
      `Directory | Jar | JavaRuntime`, so a match over them need not handle `Url`, and it
      round-trips to the text the `java.class.path` property holds: `.decodable` reads one
      from the property, and `#apply` writes one back, each using the platform's path
      separator and so needing a `System` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      val running: LocalClasspath = System.properties.java.`class`.path().as[LocalClasspath]
      val own: LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])

      own.entries.map:
        case Classpath.Entry.Directory(path) => t"directory $path"
        case Classpath.Entry.Jar(path)       => t"archive $path"
        case Classpath.Entry.JavaRuntime     => t"the Java runtime"
      ```

      A classpath is built from entries with `apply`, from one path with `of`, and extended
      with `+ path`, which adds a directory or a JAR according to what is on disk and
      ignores an entry already present. The `classloader` and `services` extensions then
      build a loader over it or enumerate its providers.
  refer hellenism.Classpath
  refer hellenism.classloader
  entry .encodable   renders a classpath as `java.class.path` text, with the platform separator
      Needs a `System` in scope for the `path.separator` property. `JavaRuntime` entries are
      dropped, since the property has no spelling for them.
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering: `classpath⟨/x.jar:/a/b/⟩`, always with `:`
      Always available, with no `System`, so it cannot be mistaken for the platform text
      `.encodable` produces: the entries are joined with `:` whatever the platform, and
      `JavaRuntime` shows as `‹jrt›`.
  entry .decodable   reads a classpath from `java.class.path` text
      Splits the text on the platform's `path.separator` (needing a `System`, and raising
      `Property.Error` if it is unset). An element ending in `/` is a `Directory`, one ending
      in `.jar` a `Jar`, and anything else a `Directory`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      t"/classes/:/lib/app.jar".as[LocalClasspath]
      ```
  entry .of   the classpath a classloader loads from, or one of a single path
      `LocalClasspath.of(classloader)` is the classpath a `URLClassLoader` over local entries
      loads from, found through `Classloader#classpath`, and otherwise the `java.class.path`
      property. Code which launches a JVM to run its own classes should take its classpath
      from this, with the classloader of one of its own classes
      (`LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Main.type])`): under a host such as a test runner,
      which isolates each suite in a `URLClassLoader`, the property names the host's own
      jars, not the code's.

      `LocalClasspath.of(path)` is the classpath of one entry, by its path: a `Directory` or a
      `Jar` according to what is on disk (so it may raise `Path.Error` or `Io.Error`, and needs
      a `DereferenceSymlinks` policy). It is `of`, not `apply`, because a single
      `Classpath.Entry` is itself a path, and `LocalClasspath(entry)` would be ambiguous with
      the entries' `apply`.
  entry .apply   a classpath of the given entries, in order
      Takes any number of `Classpath.Entry.Directory`, `Classpath.Entry.Jar` and
      `Classpath.Entry.JavaRuntime` entries, in search order. For a single path, use `of`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      LocalClasspath(Classpath.Entry.Jar(t"/x.jar"), Classpath.Entry.Directory(t"/a/b/"))
      ```
  entry .paths   `classpath + path`: adds a directory or JAR, by what is on disk
      The `Addable` given which extends a classpath with any path type. The path is read as a
      `Path on Linux` and checked on disk: a directory becomes a `Directory` entry, anything
      else a `Jar`, and an entry already present leaves the classpath as it was. It needs a
      `Tactic[Path.Error]`, a `Tactic[Io.Error]` and a `DereferenceSymlinks` policy in scope.
  entry #entries   the entries, in search order, typed to exclude URLs
  entry #entrySet   the entries as a set, for the membership check `+ path` makes
  entry #apply   the classpath as `java.class.path` text, with the platform separator
      Needs a `System` in scope for the `path.separator` property. `Directory` and `Jar`
      entries are joined in order; `JavaRuntime` is dropped, since the property has no
      spelling for it.
